{
  "_comment": "Antigravity (agy) permission baseline. Merged by setup.sh into ~/.gemini/antigravity-cli/settings.json via claude/scripts/agy-apply-permissions.py (that file also holds trustedWorkspaces and per-machine grants, so it is never symlinked). Rules are word-by-word prefixes using unified command(...) syntax (agy retired unsandboxed(...) rules). Precedence: deny > ask > allow. Security model: the terminal sandbox is the boundary (toolPermission=proceed-in-sandbox, enableTerminalSandbox=true); it blocks ~/.ssh and .env files by default and isolates the filesystem and network. Routine git, gh PR/issue, and review-gate commands are in allow so day-to-day development works like Claude Code without constant prompts, while destructive or untrusted-code commands (rm -rf, git reset --hard, git branch -D, git push --force-with-lease, git clean, git rebase, bun install, bunx, npx, curl, wget, gh api, git clone, git -C, git -c, gh auth) stay in ask and force-push / sudo / credential reads stay in deny. Reads outside the workspace are served inside the sandbox through allowNonWorkspaceAccess and read_file(~/...) rules, which the merge script expands to the machine's home; never add read_file(~/...) rules for $HOME symlinks that resolve into ~/dev/ (such as ~/.bash_aliases or ~/.gitconfig), because bwrap resolves realpath on read_file rules and mounts them read-only on top of the read-write ~/dev/ mount. read_file denies cover credential files exactly, because the file tool matches paths, not shell text. Withdrawing a rule from the permissions object does not withdraw it from a machine that already installed it, because apply is additive; the retired array lists grants the baseline has revoked. apply deletes any exact match in retired from allow, migrates/strips legacy unsandboxed(...) rules, and removes any ask entry that has been promoted to allow; check fails until retired or legacy unsandboxed rules are gone. There are deliberately no command regexes: shell syntax cannot be classified airtight (see the reverted #411), so command denies are plain prefixes that act as speed bumps inside the sandbox, not as a boundary.",
